WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.240
For months, Israel has been divided by Prime Minister Binyamin Netanyahu’s...

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.240
...attempts to weaken the judiciary

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.480
But as the country faces war with Hamas, some of those differences have been set aside

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.840
Netanyahu has formed a unity government...

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.200
...and for the time being sidelined the far-right players in his coalition

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.120
But after the war, will that help him remain in office?

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.960
I think you should certainly never count Netanyahu out

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.640
That is something that we have all learned over the past decade and a half

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.600
But I do think it is hard to see how he emerges from this politically

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.880
We’ve seen some very striking scenes over the past few days...

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.680
...something that a lot of Israelis have been pointing out...

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.520
...is that the Israeli government has been almost invisible since Saturday

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.440
Ministers, you would expect them to be visiting hospitals...

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.120
...visiting the wounded, visiting people who can’t return to their homes...

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.640
...because their homes were destroyed

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.560
But there hasn’t been much of that happening...

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.800
...and we know why, because the handful of ministerial visits that have taken place...

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.680
...we’ve seen very angry videos of ordinary Israelis...

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.600
...shouting down members of this government

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.640
There is just tremendous public anger...

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.480
...not just Netanyahu himself, but also at the government

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.320
I’ve heard this in talking to Israelis...

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.960
...you’ve had people who are right-wing, people who are religious in Israel...

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.600
...who have noticed over the past few days...

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.240
...that the people being called up to serve in the army now

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.920
The people who rushed on Saturday to try and free besieged kibbutzim...

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.560
...in southern Israel and help survivors of the massacre

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.800
Some of them were left-wingers...

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.760
...and so this idea that Netanyahu has pushed for many, many years...

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:52.640
...that the Israeli left is unpatriotic and it undermines Israel’s security...

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.160
...I think people have realised that that might not be true over the past few days

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.160
Netanyahu will try and do what he always does...

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.280
...which is shift the blame to someone else

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.640
He will blame the generals and the spy chiefs for an intelligence failure

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.440
And certainly they deserve some of the blame

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.840
And I am sure there will be a commission of inquiry...

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.640
...that looks into what went wrong, but I don’t think it’s going to work this time

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.080
I don’t think Netanyahu is going to be able to shift...

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.440
...all the blame away from himself

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.440
Any positive change would start with new political leadership on both sides

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.160
I think there is a bit of optimism that this will move Israeli politics...

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.520
...in a more centrist direction...

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.360
...and that would be a good thing for an Israel...

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.640
...that has been torn apart by political tumult over the past year

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.080
&lt;i&gt;And so I think in terms of domestic politics...&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.200
&lt;i&gt;...it might help to moderate the trend of recent years...&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.400
&lt;i&gt;...but when it comes to the Palestinians...&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.240
&lt;i&gt;...I think it will nudge Israeli politics in the other direction&lt;/i&gt;

